00:03:45.516 --> 00:03:46.890
got to get that
prototyping done.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:48.240
So they've got to
come up with some kind

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:50.073
of an approach they're
going to use to build

00:03:50.073 --> 00:03:52.264
this machine learning model.

00:03:52.264 --> 00:03:54.430
And then, they actually
have to physically build it.

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:54.640
Right?

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:57.220
They've got to build it,
train it, even on a small data

00:03:57.220 --> 00:03:59.240
center or local
environment, but they've

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:02.240
got to do something to
actually create this model.

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:05.640
But then, once that model
is trained and ready to go,

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:08.917
how do they get it
out into production?

00:04:08.917 --> 00:04:11.250
And that's where the data
engineer comes back into play,

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:13.541
because it's not only important
just to build the model

00:04:13.541 --> 00:04:14.550
and know that it works.

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:16.600
We've also got to run
this thing at scale,

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:19.464
so it can support the
application downstream.

00:04:19.464 --> 00:04:21.589
So that data engineer is
going to deploy that model

00:04:21.589 --> 00:04:24.080
to production, and
then the last step

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:27.620
is going to be integrating
that prediction mechanism

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:29.242
with the applications.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:34.330
All right, so let's start
with our first set of tasks.

00:04:34.330 --> 00:04:38.954
How do we transform and
understand data inside of GCP?

00:04:38.954 --> 00:04:40.370
So remember, it's
that first chunk

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:42.440
of that timeline with the
data engineer and the business

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:42.940
analyst.

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:47.640
So there's three main
steps that typically

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:51.090
fall into this bucket,
and I'm sure this is not

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:52.007
completely exhaustive.

00:04:52.007 --> 00:04:54.340
There's typically a lot more
work that might go on here,

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:56.730
but these are three of the
biggest pillars that we often

00:04:56.730 --> 00:04:57.865
run into.

00:04:57.865 --> 00:04:59.240
So the first step
is really going

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:03.260
to be acquiring that data,
grabbing all that raw data,

00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:05.690
and storing and in some kind
of object storage mechanism.

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:07.106
Something that's
nice and scalable

00:05:07.106 --> 00:05:10.705
that we can access from a
number of different tools.

00:05:10.705 --> 00:05:13.330
Then, we'll have to turn all of
those flat files into something

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:15.891
useful, put some
structure on that data.

00:05:15.891 --> 00:05:17.890
Cleanse it, transform it,
turn it into something

00:05:17.890 --> 00:05:19.870
where we can actually
run queries against it

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:22.417
and start learning
from the data set.

00:05:22.417 --> 00:05:24.000
And after that
transformation is done,

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:25.500
we actually want
to put it somewhere

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:27.260
where we can access
it, so typically

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:30.050
inside of a large
scale data warehouse.

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:32.090
So this is day three of
Next, so you probably

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:34.910
can guess which tools I'm
going to show you here.

00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:38.130
So for acquiring and storing
that data in object storage,

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:41.840
GCS, Google Cloud Storage,
is a great fit for that.

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:44.279
For transforming the flat
files and the structured data,

00:05:44.279 --> 00:05:46.070
we actually have a
bunch of different tools

00:05:46.070 --> 00:05:48.170
we can use here, but
I like using Dataprep.

00:05:48.170 --> 00:05:50.180
Mostly because it's
a nice visual way

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:52.409
to sort of dig around and
understand sample data,

00:05:52.409 --> 00:05:54.200
and we'll talk through
a little bit of that

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:56.220
as we get further in.

00:05:56.220 --> 00:06:00.000
Then, the last piece is BigQuery
petabyte scale, analytical data

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.440
warehouse, a perfect
place for ad hoc queries.

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:05.370
Again, we're in understanding
and model-building phase.

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:06.840
So it's early on.

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:09.870
This doesn't have to serve any
kind of transactional traffic

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:10.590
or anything.

00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:12.990
All we're focused on
doing is giving ourselves

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:15.090
access to query and
understand the data.

00:06:17.650 --> 00:06:20.110
So if we dig into each one
of those just a little bit,

00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:23.130
this is an example of
what GCS looks like.

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:25.422
It's just a nice bucket
folder-style approach,

00:06:25.422 --> 00:06:27.130
where I've created a
bucket in this case.

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:29.670
And the data set
I'm using, as we're

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:32.430
working through this example,
is like the MovieLens data set.

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:35.235
So I grabbed the 20 million
rating MovieLens data

00:06:35.235 --> 00:06:38.770
set, unzipped it, dropped all
the CSV's into Google Cloud

00:06:38.770 --> 00:06:39.270
Storage.

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:42.180
So now, I've got the
raw data sitting here.

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:44.280
So the next step
is transformation.

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:46.110
That's where Dataprep comes in.

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:49.830
Now, as I mentioned, Dataprep
is a great data wrangling tool.

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:53.490
It lets you pull in from
Cloud Storage or BigQuery,

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:56.490
transform all the data you want
to transform against, and then

00:06:56.490 --> 00:06:59.280
actually dump that data
back out into Cloud Storage

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:00.660
or into BigQuery.

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:03.980
So it gives you that
input and output option.

00:07:03.980 --> 00:07:05.720
What's really cool
about Dataprep

00:07:05.720 --> 00:07:09.710
is that, behind the scenes,
the transformations that you

00:07:09.710 --> 00:07:12.650
define, the steps that you
take, the filters you run,

00:07:12.650 --> 00:07:15.980
are actually packaged up
into an Apache Beam job,

00:07:15.980 --> 00:07:19.490
and that Beam job is actually
pushed to the Dataflow service.

00:07:19.490 --> 00:07:23.120
So the actual execution
mechanism here is actually

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:25.760
Cloud Dataflow
behind the scenes,

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:28.845
and now the inputs and
outputs here with Dataprep

00:07:28.845 --> 00:07:29.720
are somewhat limited.

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:32.070
This doesn't cover the
breadth of the platform.

00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:36.110
If you need a level deeper of
maybe transformations, or maybe

00:07:36.110 --> 00:07:39.260
you want to get through
inputting from Cloud Datastore

00:07:39.260 --> 00:07:42.320
and outputting to a Cloud
SQL or Cloud Spanner,

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:44.360
that's where you want
to incorporate Dataflow.

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:46.817
But for our use case,
Dataprep works perfectly well,

00:07:46.817 --> 00:07:48.650
because our data is
living in Cloud Storage,

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:53.490
and we want to make sure it
lands in BigQuery eventually.

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:56.470
So this is an example
of the Dataprep UI,

00:07:56.470 --> 00:07:58.110
and what you'll
see-- and I get I'll

00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:00.276
go through this a little
bit more in-depth as we get

00:08:00.276 --> 00:08:02.310
to the demo part-- but
what I want you to notice

00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:04.680
is that we've got this
row and column setup.

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:08.010
This is a CSV that I imported,
and this is the cleanse kind

00:08:08.010 --> 00:08:09.897
of ready-to-go output.

00:08:09.897 --> 00:08:12.480
But what you'll see across the
top there, in those green bars,

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:15.210
are actually Dataprep
sampling the entire column

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:18.100
and showing me a distribution
of all possible values there.

00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:22.320
And so that kind of tooling
makes the analysis process

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:23.190
much easier.

00:08:23.190 --> 00:08:25.332
It helps me figure out
which of these data sets

00:08:25.332 --> 00:08:26.790
do I want to worry
about, which are

00:08:26.790 --> 00:08:28.360
these columns do I care about?

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:30.995
Which ones do I want to
potentially transform or join,

00:08:30.995 --> 00:08:33.120
as I'm thinking about what
I'm going to give access

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:34.650
to my business analyst.

00:08:37.709 --> 00:08:39.250
And so here's what
a flow looks like.

00:08:39.250 --> 00:08:41.600
So in the example I'm
going to show you,

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:44.179
I have the MovieLens
20 million rating data

00:08:44.179 --> 00:08:46.730
So I've got the movies data
set up at the upper left.

00:08:46.730 --> 00:08:48.710
The next one is ratings.

00:08:48.710 --> 00:08:53.090
The Links file includes like
the movie database link or ID

00:08:53.090 --> 00:08:55.460
and the IMDB ID for the movie.

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:58.070
And then the last one, in the
very bottom there, is the tags,

00:08:58.070 --> 00:09:00.184
and I didn't really worry
too much about tags.

00:09:00.184 --> 00:09:01.850
But what you'll see
up here is that I've

00:09:01.850 --> 00:09:05.270
taken the movies
portion of the data set,

00:09:05.270 --> 00:09:07.850
and the first thing I did
was clean it up a little bit.

00:09:07.850 --> 00:09:11.330
And so things like genres were
pipe separated text fields,

00:09:11.330 --> 00:09:13.220
and I wanted to make
that JSON array.

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:14.810
So I was able to do
that inside there,

00:09:14.810 --> 00:09:17.360
as well as do things
like extract the year out

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:20.590
of the title and make
that its own column.

00:09:20.590 --> 00:09:22.180
Then, I was actually
able to join it

00:09:22.180 --> 00:09:25.250
with other data sets inside
of this whole large group.

00:09:25.250 --> 00:09:28.330
So I took the ratings,
and I actually

00:09:28.330 --> 00:09:30.550
created an average
rating, so kind

00:09:30.550 --> 00:09:33.020
of a little bit of a swoop
down to the bottom there.

00:09:33.020 --> 00:09:35.320
But I was able to compute
the average ratings per movie

00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:39.290
from that 20 million set and
then push those average ratings

00:09:39.290 --> 00:09:41.740
and join them up
back with the movies.

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:44.370
And so the nice thing here
is that the movies data

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:47.360
set itself was actually
able to be enriched,

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:49.664
all done inside of this tool.

00:09:49.664 --> 00:09:50.830
And so all of those little--

00:09:50.830 --> 00:09:52.110
I know they are a
little bit hard to see--

00:09:52.110 --> 00:09:54.568
but all those little arrows
that are pointing to the right,

00:09:54.568 --> 00:09:57.020
at the end of every step,
those are actually the job.

00:09:57.020 --> 00:09:58.500
That's the output component.

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:00.314
So that's where I
get to decide, where

00:10:00.314 --> 00:10:01.730
do I want to put
this data out to?

00:10:01.730 --> 00:10:03.880
Do I want to append it
to an existing table

00:10:03.880 --> 00:10:06.102
inside of BigQuery or
replace the data set?

00:10:06.102 --> 00:10:08.060
So you have a lot of
flexibility with Dataprep.

00:10:08.060 --> 00:10:10.380
It gives you a lot of
transformational power

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:12.261
around individual
data sets and how

00:10:12.261 --> 00:10:14.510
you might want to work with
them, join them, use them.

00:10:17.480 --> 00:10:21.380
So once that's set of jobs
runs, all my data lands inside

00:10:21.380 --> 00:10:24.110
of BigQuery, and here's just a
