Screen shot's not very exciting.

00:10:35.553 --> 00:10:38.900
I'll show you more when we
actually run some queries.

00:10:38.900 --> 00:10:41.390
But here's a quick
screenshot of what

00:10:41.390 --> 00:10:45.840
that looks like inside the
BigQuery UI, so you can see.

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:48.870
We can run standard SQL
queries against this data now.

00:10:48.870 --> 00:10:52.410
So any SQL capabilities you
might have for your business

00:10:52.410 --> 00:10:55.290
analyst, they're able to go
ahead and run ad hoc queries

00:10:55.290 --> 00:10:56.951
immediately against
this data set.

00:10:56.951 --> 00:10:58.950
So we're already making
a little bit of progress

00:10:58.950 --> 00:11:01.636
through the work that we've
got to do to get to build

00:11:01.636 --> 00:11:02.760
that recommendation system.

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:08.060
So if we want to
get a level deeper

00:11:08.060 --> 00:11:10.010
on exploring and
understanding data,

00:11:10.010 --> 00:11:11.717
I think SQL is a
really powerful tool,

00:11:11.717 --> 00:11:14.300
but it's not the right tool for
every single person out there.

00:11:14.300 --> 00:11:18.740
And there are an incredibly huge
number of business intelligence

00:11:18.740 --> 00:11:21.456
tools out there that we want
to be able to plug into.

00:11:21.456 --> 00:11:22.830
So the great thing
about BigQuery

00:11:22.830 --> 00:11:25.800
is that it does support
things like Tableau and Looker

00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:28.040
and our own Data Studio product.

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:30.220
So that you can build
things, like reports,

00:11:30.220 --> 00:11:33.360
dashboards, build kind
of exploratory analyzes,

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:35.110
of the data that
you're looking at.

00:11:35.110 --> 00:11:37.050
So it's another nice
way to think about, how

00:11:37.050 --> 00:11:38.910
can I examine and
understand this data,

00:11:38.910 --> 00:11:40.380
because that's really what
we're trying to get to.

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:42.180
We're trying to make
sure we can develop

00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:44.730
a really intense, in-depth
understanding, if we're

00:11:44.730 --> 00:11:47.250
trying to build a predictive
component on top of it.

00:11:49.998 --> 00:11:53.180
All right, so we've finished
the first major set of tasks.

00:11:53.180 --> 00:11:53.680
Right?

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:55.990
We acquired the data set,
and we've transformed it

00:11:55.990 --> 00:11:56.926
into something useful.

00:11:56.926 --> 00:11:58.300
We've got a bunch
of tools we can

00:11:58.300 --> 00:12:00.250
use to really develop
an understanding,

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:02.140
but how do we make
the next leap?

00:12:02.140 --> 00:12:06.700
How do we move into building
a machine learning model?

00:12:06.700 --> 00:12:08.760
So that's where the
data scientist comes in.

00:12:08.760 --> 00:12:11.190
If you remember, we
have two big tasks here.

00:12:11.190 --> 00:12:12.999
One, we've got to
prototype the approach,

00:12:12.999 --> 00:12:15.540
and the second is we actually
have to build the model itself.

00:12:19.040 --> 00:12:21.910
So the components that you'll
need to accomplish those tasks

00:12:21.910 --> 00:12:22.957
for a data scientist.

00:12:22.957 --> 00:12:25.540
One, obviously, you're going to
need a pretty powerful machine

00:12:25.540 --> 00:12:28.030
learning framework, something
that you can lean on heavily

00:12:28.030 --> 00:12:29.881
to support this kind of work.

00:12:29.881 --> 00:12:31.630
And then, you actually
need an environment

00:12:31.630 --> 00:12:36.130
to physically prototype
the approach within.

00:12:36.130 --> 00:12:39.400
So inside of GCP we have two
good options there for you.

00:12:39.400 --> 00:12:42.130
TensorFlow, which is
a scalable, machine

00:12:42.130 --> 00:12:45.550
learning library and
framework, and Cloud Datalab,

00:12:45.550 --> 00:12:48.700
which you can just imagine as
Jupyter notebooks or IPython

00:12:48.700 --> 00:12:49.770
notebooks as a service.

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:54.140
So let's dig in a little bit.

00:12:54.140 --> 00:12:57.460
TensorFlow is
incredibly powerful.

00:12:57.460 --> 00:12:58.900
It's also relatively
complicated,

00:12:58.900 --> 00:13:00.490
and I'm a TensorFlow
newbie myself,

00:13:00.490 --> 00:13:02.800
so this took me a little bit
of work to get all this up

00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:04.180
and running.

00:13:04.180 --> 00:13:07.540
But think of it as a
powerful open source

00:13:07.540 --> 00:13:09.940
library for really
high performance

00:13:09.940 --> 00:13:13.012
numerical computation.

00:13:13.012 --> 00:13:14.470
The great thing
about TensorFlow is

00:13:14.470 --> 00:13:17.950
I think it establishes
a really nice baseline.

00:13:17.950 --> 00:13:20.260
And what you're seeing
within the ecosystem now

00:13:20.260 --> 00:13:22.597
is a lot of stuff that's
sitting on top of TensorFlow,

00:13:22.597 --> 00:13:25.180
higher level abstractions that
are making it easier and easier

00:13:25.180 --> 00:13:26.170
to work with.

00:13:26.170 --> 00:13:28.180
And TensorFlow
itself is improving

00:13:28.180 --> 00:13:31.690
at an incredible pace.

00:13:31.690 --> 00:13:34.450
It actually is able
to run on a number

00:13:34.450 --> 00:13:36.250
of different environments
though as well.

00:13:36.250 --> 00:13:42.280
It can run on just bare CPUs,
GPUs, or even our own Tensor

00:13:42.280 --> 00:13:46.940
Processing Units or TPUs that
are available inside of GCP,

00:13:46.940 --> 00:13:48.602
and it can be deployed.

00:13:48.602 --> 00:13:50.060
Those models can
be deployed to run

00:13:50.060 --> 00:13:53.990
on anything from mobile
devices to large scale VM

00:13:53.990 --> 00:13:55.100
infrastructure.

00:13:55.100 --> 00:13:58.250
So it really is a very
capable framework,

00:13:58.250 --> 00:14:04.030
and there is an incredible
number of blog posts, how-tos,

00:14:04.030 --> 00:14:06.970
guides, tutorials out there,
all the way from formal

00:14:06.970 --> 00:14:09.370
to informal, of how
to use TensorFlow

00:14:09.370 --> 00:14:13.220
for different
classes of problem.

00:14:13.220 --> 00:14:15.790
So we've got a good
framework we can build on.

00:14:15.790 --> 00:14:18.130
Well now, we need in an
environment to prototype it in,

00:14:18.130 --> 00:14:20.260
and that's where, again,
Cloud Datalab comes in.

00:14:20.260 --> 00:14:24.610
And think of Datalab as just a
wide open notebook interface,

00:14:24.610 --> 00:14:26.260
but we've actually
tweaked the Jupyter

00:14:26.260 --> 00:14:27.885
and the IPython
components a little bit

00:14:27.885 --> 00:14:30.440
to give you access to
some really simple tools.

00:14:30.440 --> 00:14:33.301
So for example,
inside that notebook,

00:14:33.301 --> 00:14:35.050
we give you quick
access to running things

00:14:35.050 --> 00:14:38.770
like BigQuery queries or looking
at files out of Google Cloud

00:14:38.770 --> 00:14:41.920
Storage without having to
import individual libraries

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:43.310
and set those up.

00:14:43.310 --> 00:14:45.070
So we try to make it
as easy as possible,

00:14:45.070 --> 00:14:49.000
and a first-class citizen
amongst all the other data

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:51.900
services that you
might want to leverage.

00:14:51.900 --> 00:14:54.170
But inside those notebooks,
you can explore that data.

00:14:54.170 --> 00:14:55.810
You can, obviously, analyze it.

00:14:55.810 --> 00:14:58.290
Visualization components
are built right in,

00:14:58.290 --> 00:15:01.970
and it's actually perfectly
suited to building machine

00:15:01.970 --> 00:15:03.140
learning models.

00:15:03.140 --> 00:15:06.530
And it actually plugs directly
into using TensorFlow and even

00:15:06.530 --> 00:15:08.090
deploying those models later on.

00:15:11.220 --> 00:15:15.200
So here's an example of what
the Cloud Datalab UI looks like,

00:15:15.200 --> 00:15:16.700
again, very
notebook-driven, but you

00:15:16.700 --> 00:15:19.630
can see we've got a mix of
I'm running some queries.

00:15:19.630 --> 00:15:22.405
I'm charting some of the output,
I'm importing some Python

00:15:22.405 --> 00:15:24.530
libraries, and I'm actually
writing a function here

00:15:24.530 --> 00:15:25.130
for training.

00:15:29.340 --> 00:15:32.300
So now, we've got the
first four bits done.

00:15:32.300 --> 00:15:34.790
We acquired the data,
we transformed it

00:15:34.790 --> 00:15:38.414
into something useful, we were
able to prototype our approach

00:15:38.414 --> 00:15:40.580
with a machine learning
framework in an environment,

00:15:40.580 --> 00:15:43.836
and we were able to
start building our model.

00:15:43.836 --> 00:15:46.460
Now that that's all done, how do
we-- maybe we even trained it,

00:15:46.460 --> 00:15:49.130
in that case, in a small,
constrained environment.

00:15:49.130 --> 00:15:52.370
But now, how do we train it and
deploy it and run it at scale

00:15:52.370 --> 00:15:56.349
and really incorporate
the usage of that model?

00:15:56.349 --> 00:15:58.640
So we've got the next step
here is deploying that model

00:15:58.640 --> 00:16:02.490
and training and deploying
it and running it at scale.

00:16:02.490 --> 00:16:04.490
So for the data
engineer, there's

00:16:04.490 --> 00:16:06.760
two main steps
they've got to take.

00:16:06.760 --> 00:16:09.850
First thing is they've got to
push that model to the cloud

00:16:09.850 --> 00:16:12.250
to train it and then
deploy it, ultimately,

00:16:12.250 --> 00:16:14.259
on a running service
that can scale.

00:16:14.259 --> 00:16:16.300
And then they've got to
integrate that prediction

00:16:16.300 --> 00:16:20.280
mechanism into an application.

00:16:20.280 --> 00:16:21.907
So the steps that
we have there are--

00:16:21.907 --> 00:16:23.490
or the tools that
we want to use there

00:16:23.490 --> 00:16:28.280
are Cloud Machine Learning
Engine and our Cloud APIs

00:16:28.280 --> 00:16:30.170
which are basically
just the endpoint you're

00:16:30.170 --> 00:16:32.150
going to use here.

00:16:32.150 --> 00:16:34.830
Cloud Machine Learning Engine
is an incredibly compelling

00:16:34.830 --> 00:16:37.740
product, because it takes
your TensorFlow model,

00:16:37.740 --> 00:16:39.750
or it takes your
training task, and it

00:16:39.750 --> 00:16:44.850
lets you run it on a much
larger set of infrastructure.

00:16:44.850 --> 00:16:48.090
So you can run things like
your TensorFlow models

00:16:48.090 --> 00:16:49.710
at an incredible
scale without having

00:16:49.710 --> 00:16:52.075
to worry about spinning
up those machines,

00:16:52.075 --> 00:16:54.450
configuring them, installing
all the rest of the required

00:16:54.450 --> 00:16:57.280
software, coordinating
the work that they do.

00:16:57.280 --> 00:16:59.950
TensorFlow is built
to be distributed.

00:16:59.950 --> 00:17:03.870
So we can take advantage of
that and create a large cluster

00:17:03.870 --> 00:17:04.829
and deploy the job too.

00:17:04.829 --> 00:17:07.347
So that it can complete
the training process.

00:17:07.347 --> 00:17:09.180
And then when that
training process is done,

00:17:09.180 --> 00:17:11.190
you can actually take
that completed model

00:17:11.190 --> 00:17:12.839
and push it back
up to the surface.

00:17:12.839 --> 00:17:15.060
And say, now, I want to
make this a serving model,

00:17:15.060 --> 00:17:16.829
and it gives you
back a URL endpoint

00:17:16.829 --> 00:17:18.991
that you can integrate
inside of your applications.

00:17:21.940 --> 00:17:23.760
So an example of
what that flow looks

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:26.390
like, for pushing the cloud
and training and deploying,

00:17:26.390 --> 00:17:28.140
would be-- you might
want to do some light

00:17:28.140 --> 00:17:30.660
preprocessing or heavy,
depending on your input data--

00:17:30.660 --> 00:17:33.390
but you're going to preprocess
the data set you've got.

00:17:33.390 --> 00:17:35.390
And then, you're going
to submit a training job,

00:17:35.390 --> 00:17:39.060
and that training job is a
Python TensorFlow training

00:17:39.060 --> 00:17:39.576
task.

00:17:39.576 --> 00:17:40.950
You're going to
push that job up,

00:17:40.950 --> 00:17:43.020
and there are a series
of parameters that we'll

00:17:43.020 --> 00:17:45.081
talk through quickly later on.

00:17:45.081 --> 00:17:47.580
But effectively, you're just
giving this job to the service,

00:17:47.580 --> 00:17:49.530
and saying, here's
my training task.

00:17:49.530 --> 00:17:53.090
I want you to run it across
a number of machines.

00:17:53.090 --> 00:17:54.740
Once that job is
done, it will actually

00:17:54.740 --> 00:17:56.120
have a little output model.

00:17:56.120 --> 00:17:57.578
And you're going
to take that model

00:17:57.578 --> 00:17:59.870
and say, now, create an
entry for that serving

00:17:59.870 --> 00:18:03.794
model inside of a CMLE,
inside of a Cloud ML engine,

00:18:03.794 --> 00:18:05.210
and then you can
create a version.

00:18:05.210 --> 00:18:06.918
So you actually have
the ability here now

00:18:06.918 --> 00:18:09.020
to take different
models you've created,

00:18:09.020 --> 00:18:12.200
attach versions to them,
and maybe even AB test them

00:18:12.200 --> 00:18:13.970
as far as like predictions go.

00:18:13.970 --> 00:18:16.050
So you have that
capability here.

00:18:16.050 --> 00:18:17.550
Once you run that
last command, it's

00:18:17.550 --> 00:18:19.420
actually going to
create that URL endpoint

00:18:19.420 --> 00:18:21.960
and give you an option
to, again, integrate that

00:18:21.960 --> 00:18:24.760
into an application.

00:18:24.760 --> 00:18:26.260
So there's two ways
you can actually

00:18:26.260 --> 00:18:29.230
test the prediction mechanism,
come in through the top.

00:18:29.230 --> 00:18:31.390
We actually have
a test capability

00:18:31.390 --> 00:18:34.720
built into the gcloud command,
inside of the gcloud SDK.

00:18:34.720 --> 00:18:37.557
So you can just run gcloud
ml-engine predict, tell it

00:18:37.557 --> 00:18:39.140
the model you want
to protect against,

00:18:39.140 --> 00:18:43.350
give it some input data,
and out pops your answer.

00:18:43.350 --> 00:18:44.820
Or you can come
in from the bottom

00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:46.560
and iterate into application.

00:18:46.560 --> 00:18:49.710
And it really is just a URL
you get to post against.

00:18:49.710 --> 00:18:51.570
This is a service
that we host for you

00:18:51.570 --> 00:18:53.070
and scale for you as needed.

00:18:53.070 --> 00:18:54.920
So that's just an
API you integrate,

00:18:54.920 --> 00:18:56.100
and you can post against.

00:18:56.100 --> 00:18:58.410
Or you actually have the
option of going a step further

00:18:58.410 --> 00:19:01.350
here and saying, I'm going
to wrap this inside of a more

00:19:01.350 --> 00:19:03.135
powerful, like Cloud
Endpoints approach,

00:19:03.135 --> 00:19:05.550
so I have a good set of
API management capabilities

00:19:05.550 --> 00:19:06.640
on top of it.

00:19:06.640 --> 00:19:08.270
It's up you.

00:19:08.270 --> 00:19:11.110
But ultimately, it's still the
same backend infrastructure,

00:19:11.110 --> 00:19:14.305
where both those commands land
you inside of Cloud ML Engine,

00:19:14.305 --> 00:19:15.930
and you get those
predictions back out.

00:19:19.349 --> 00:19:21.140
So let's put it all
together and figure out

00:19:21.140 --> 00:19:22.770
what this looks like.

00:19:22.770 --> 00:19:26.360
So you remember, we've got
these six main components--

00:19:26.360 --> 00:19:31.490
transforming, understanding,
prototyping the approach,

00:19:31.490 --> 00:19:35.240
building the model,
deploying it at scale,

00:19:35.240 --> 00:19:38.190
and integrating
with applications.

00:19:38.190 --> 00:19:41.090
So when you map
that back to GCP,

00:19:41.090 --> 00:19:44.070
you get the appropriate
kind of components here.

00:19:44.070 --> 00:19:46.610
So for transforming,
we use Dataprep.

00:19:46.610 --> 00:19:49.376
For understanding, we
would use BigQuery.

00:19:49.376 --> 00:19:51.250
For prototyping and
maybe building the model,

00:19:51.250 --> 00:19:53.289
we might use Cloud Datalab.

00:19:53.289 --> 00:19:55.080
When we deploy it,
we're going to deploy it

00:19:55.080 --> 00:19:56.490
to Cloud ML Engine.

00:19:56.490 --> 00:19:59.042
And finally, we'll use
the API that comes out,

00:19:59.042 --> 00:20:01.500
or we can, again, use Cloud
Endpoints to actually integrate

00:20:01.500 --> 00:20:03.284
with our running application.

00:20:05.804 --> 00:20:08.220
So as part of this, I did want
to walk you through as much

00:20:08.220 --> 00:20:11.676
of the UI in real world
as much as possible.

00:20:11.676 --> 00:20:13.050
So I'm going to
show, don't tell,

00:20:13.050 --> 00:20:15.390
and here's where
the demo should be.

00:20:15.390 --> 00:20:18.024
So if we could switch
to the Chromebook.

00:20:25.620 --> 00:20:26.530
All right, great.

