So we do as much as we can.

00:33:40.230 --> 00:33:40.730
Whoops.

00:33:40.730 --> 00:33:44.490
That was my fault. We'll
come back to that one.

00:33:44.490 --> 00:33:45.990
We do as much as
we can to make sure

00:33:45.990 --> 00:33:47.920
that there is a lot of
the typical libraries

00:33:47.920 --> 00:33:52.200
that you want to use baked into
the Datalab core image that's

00:33:52.200 --> 00:33:53.160
running here.

00:33:53.160 --> 00:33:55.560
But you also have the
ability to import your own

00:33:55.560 --> 00:33:58.000
or to add your own.

00:33:58.000 --> 00:34:00.360
I'll come back to that one.

00:34:00.360 --> 00:34:03.000
Let's say, I wanted to add
something else, something that

00:34:03.000 --> 00:34:07.299
wasn't in the image by default.
So there we can actually

00:34:07.299 --> 00:34:08.340
run pip commands in line.

00:34:08.340 --> 00:34:11.952
So I can put pip install.

00:34:11.952 --> 00:34:15.503
Somebody yell out an
elaboration install here.

00:34:15.503 --> 00:34:17.517
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:34:17.517 --> 00:34:19.308
SANDEEP PARIKH: I missed
that say it again.

00:34:19.308 --> 00:34:20.870
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:34:20.870 --> 00:34:22.370
SANDEEP PARIKH: I
couldn't hear you.

00:34:22.370 --> 00:34:22.870
Sorry.

00:34:22.870 --> 00:34:25.281
So I'll just pick something.

00:34:25.281 --> 00:34:26.989
I'm going to pick the
sixth library here,

00:34:26.989 --> 00:34:28.699
because it's pretty
straightforward,

00:34:28.699 --> 00:34:30.157
actually might
already be in there.

00:34:31.982 --> 00:34:34.190
All right, I picked something
that was already there,

00:34:34.190 --> 00:34:35.580
but you can see,
running pip commands

00:34:35.580 --> 00:34:36.420
is also available to you.

00:34:36.420 --> 00:34:38.545
So if you need to install
additional libraries that

00:34:38.545 --> 00:34:42.020
are not part of that core
image, you can do that as well.

00:34:42.020 --> 00:34:44.389
And then the last bit is
actually writing some Python.

00:34:44.389 --> 00:34:46.270
So I'm not going
to run this here,

00:34:46.270 --> 00:34:48.909
because there's a whole bunch
more functions that I left out.

00:34:48.909 --> 00:34:50.739
But the goal here is
you could actually

00:34:50.739 --> 00:34:53.800
create your training task
function right in line here

00:34:53.800 --> 00:34:56.139
and actually use some of
those similar directives

00:34:56.139 --> 00:34:59.740
to run gcloud commands, to
actually push the training task

00:34:59.740 --> 00:35:04.310
function up to the ML Engine
service, to actually run it.

00:35:04.310 --> 00:35:06.640
And so I previously
did some of that work,

00:35:06.640 --> 00:35:11.360
and let me take a quick look and
show you what that looks like.

00:35:16.967 --> 00:35:19.050
So here's an example of
what it looked like for me

00:35:19.050 --> 00:35:23.620
to submit the training
job that I had earlier.

00:35:23.620 --> 00:35:25.690
So up at the top, I
just defined a handful

00:35:25.690 --> 00:35:29.275
of environment variables, where
the input data is where I want

00:35:29.275 --> 00:35:30.850
it to dump some of the output.

00:35:30.850 --> 00:35:32.620
In this case, this
data was preprocessed,

00:35:32.620 --> 00:35:34.900
and I'm using actually a sample
that I'll tell you about in

00:35:34.900 --> 00:35:35.399
a minute.

00:35:35.399 --> 00:35:37.990
But this sample actually
takes the 20 million

00:35:37.990 --> 00:35:40.480
rating and MovieLens
data set, and it trains

00:35:40.480 --> 00:35:41.980
a couple of different models.

00:35:41.980 --> 00:35:44.860
It does one for a
deep learning model

00:35:44.860 --> 00:35:48.230
and another for a matrix
factorization model.

00:35:48.230 --> 00:35:51.884
And when you look down
here at this command, what

00:35:51.884 --> 00:35:54.050
you're actually seeing is
there's two different sets

00:35:54.050 --> 00:35:55.080
of arguments.

00:35:55.080 --> 00:35:57.830
The first set, the
first set of arguments

00:35:57.830 --> 00:36:01.010
are the actual ones I need to
pass to the ML Engine service.

00:36:01.010 --> 00:36:03.200
So things like what is
the actual Python module

00:36:03.200 --> 00:36:04.350
name of the task?

00:36:04.350 --> 00:36:06.290
In this case, it's trainer.task.

00:36:06.290 --> 00:36:08.120
Where is the path
to that package?

00:36:08.120 --> 00:36:10.760
It's right in line in the
same directory I'm in.

00:36:10.760 --> 00:36:13.370
Where do I want to stage
the intermediate output?

00:36:13.370 --> 00:36:14.870
Which region do I
want to run it in?

00:36:14.870 --> 00:36:16.911
And what are some of the
configuration parameters

00:36:16.911 --> 00:36:18.014
that I want to have there?

00:36:18.014 --> 00:36:19.430
We can specify all
those, and then

00:36:19.430 --> 00:36:21.740
the second set of arguments
are actually arguments

00:36:21.740 --> 00:36:24.260
to my trainer file
itself, to my Python task.

00:36:24.260 --> 00:36:27.560
So here, I'm giving it things
like where should I be putting

00:36:27.560 --> 00:36:30.200
some of the output data?

00:36:30.200 --> 00:36:34.040
What about the evaluation type
or maybe some of the other

00:36:34.040 --> 00:36:36.725
features that I'm
trying to expose here?

00:36:36.725 --> 00:36:38.600
And then, I'm also doing
the exact same thing

00:36:38.600 --> 00:36:41.750
for the factorisation
model further down the way,

00:36:41.750 --> 00:36:43.887
and this is submitting
it in async fashion.

00:36:43.887 --> 00:36:45.470
So you had that
option with ML Engine.

00:36:45.470 --> 00:36:48.060
You could either submit in
synchronous or asynchronous.

00:36:48.060 --> 00:36:49.490
If you do it with
sync, it streams

00:36:49.490 --> 00:36:50.930
the logs from your
training output

00:36:50.930 --> 00:36:52.190
right back to your console.

00:36:52.190 --> 00:36:54.469
Or if you do it async, it
gives you back a job ID,

00:36:54.469 --> 00:36:56.510
and says, go ahead and
check this job ID later on

00:36:56.510 --> 00:36:58.290
to see how it progressed.

00:37:03.580 --> 00:37:06.670
Now, if we take a look
at the configuration,

00:37:06.670 --> 00:37:10.240
just to show you really quickly,
first thing I do is show you.

00:37:15.650 --> 00:37:18.010
So this is one of the input
configuration files we use.

00:37:18.010 --> 00:37:19.600
And this configuration
bit is actually

00:37:19.600 --> 00:37:21.891
these are arguments you can
specify on the command line

00:37:21.891 --> 00:37:23.524
if you want to or not.

00:37:23.524 --> 00:37:24.940
But what they're
basically telling

00:37:24.940 --> 00:37:27.724
ML Engine is how do you
want me to run your job?

00:37:27.724 --> 00:37:29.890
So in this case, the scale
tier I picked-- and there

00:37:29.890 --> 00:37:31.780
are different ones, but
effectively, here you

00:37:31.780 --> 00:37:33.790
have the option of
saying, I need something

00:37:33.790 --> 00:37:36.139
at the most basic level,
so maybe a set of CPUs.

00:37:36.139 --> 00:37:37.930
Maybe I want to run on
the, quote, unquote,

00:37:37.930 --> 00:37:41.230
"premium level," which is I
think one master and about

00:37:41.230 --> 00:37:44.050
10 workers with GPUs.

00:37:44.050 --> 00:37:46.700
Or I can run with TPUs as well.

00:37:46.700 --> 00:37:48.790
So you have a lot of
flexibility to define

00:37:48.790 --> 00:37:52.030
how much you want done here,
how many components you

00:37:52.030 --> 00:37:55.734
want to incorporate
into your training set.

00:37:55.734 --> 00:37:57.400
And actually, one
thing I didn't mention

00:37:57.400 --> 00:37:59.108
that I will mention
here is at the bottom

00:37:59.108 --> 00:38:02.230
there you see a section
called Parameters, or params.

00:38:02.230 --> 00:38:05.530
And ML Engine offers
this really cool notion

00:38:05.530 --> 00:38:09.190
of hypertuning your parameters,
and what that means is you

00:38:09.190 --> 00:38:12.094
get to tell us some bounds
on certain parameters.

00:38:12.094 --> 00:38:14.260
In this case, I'm going to
talk about learning rate.

00:38:14.260 --> 00:38:18.220
There's a Min and Max, and
what I want ML Engine to do

00:38:18.220 --> 00:38:20.260
is run multiple
permutations of this

00:38:20.260 --> 00:38:23.590
and tell me what's the right
value for learning rate.

00:38:23.590 --> 00:38:25.930
So when it finishes this
training, one of the outputs

00:38:25.930 --> 00:38:28.000
here is not just the
model, but it's actually

00:38:28.000 --> 00:38:29.560
going to tell me
the optimum value I

00:38:29.560 --> 00:38:31.540
should use for a learning rate.

00:38:31.540 --> 00:38:33.520
So there's a lot
of power just baked

00:38:33.520 --> 00:38:35.920
into this mechanism,
where you give it a task,

00:38:35.920 --> 00:38:37.840
you tell it which
parameters are flexible,

00:38:37.840 --> 00:38:40.300
and that you want to get
the most optimal outcome

00:38:40.300 --> 00:38:41.367
for those parameters.

00:38:45.066 --> 00:38:46.940
So we could have submitted
that training job,

00:38:46.940 --> 00:38:49.229
and when we submit
it, it goes into-- oh,

00:38:49.229 --> 00:38:50.270
that's a little too big--

00:38:50.270 --> 00:38:52.419
it goes into the
ML Engine UI here.

00:38:52.419 --> 00:38:54.710
And I was doing a bunch of
training and breaking things

00:38:54.710 --> 00:38:55.501
all over the place.

00:38:55.501 --> 00:38:59.491
So here's one example of the
matrix factorization job.

00:38:59.491 --> 00:38:59.990
I ran it.

00:38:59.990 --> 00:39:01.781
It took about two hours,
because I was only

00:39:01.781 --> 00:39:05.070
running it on a single master
with a couple of workers.

00:39:05.070 --> 00:39:07.070
And you can see all the
inputs that it gave you,

00:39:07.070 --> 00:39:09.240
and the training output
based on the hyperparameters

00:39:09.240 --> 00:39:09.740
you gave it.

00:39:09.740 --> 00:39:11.960
So now, it's showing me,
for every single run,

00:39:11.960 --> 00:39:15.950
every single trial, what the
learning rate it used there

00:39:15.950 --> 00:39:20.058
and what the optimal
outcome might have been.

00:39:20.058 --> 00:39:22.390
And if you actually were
to look at this in line

00:39:22.390 --> 00:39:23.950
when it's running,
you would actually

00:39:23.950 --> 00:39:27.810
see a breakdown of every
worker and the CPU usage

00:39:27.810 --> 00:39:29.600
for across all
them, the ram usage.

00:39:29.600 --> 00:39:32.016
So you get a sense of what's
actually physically happening

00:39:32.016 --> 00:39:35.990
behind the scenes, when we're
running those jobs themselves.

00:39:35.990 --> 00:39:38.210
But once that
training is completed,

00:39:38.210 --> 00:39:40.800
we actually have to deploy
the model to production.

00:39:40.800 --> 00:39:43.520
So that's where I'll show
you the final step here.

00:39:50.270 --> 00:39:52.770
That's actually in the previous
file that we're showing you.

00:39:52.770 --> 00:39:55.287
But it's the component
where I could actually just

00:39:55.287 --> 00:39:56.870
run again a series
of gcloud commands,

00:39:56.870 --> 00:40:00.780
actually deploy that model to
a production scaling service.

00:40:00.780 --> 00:40:03.060
And when you actually run
a couple of those commands,

00:40:03.060 --> 00:40:05.890
you get a model in the
model section of the UI.

00:40:05.890 --> 00:40:08.650
So in my matrix
factorization model,

00:40:08.650 --> 00:40:11.250
I created a default
version, which is v1,

00:40:11.250 --> 00:40:14.932
and I now have the
ability to use that model

00:40:14.932 --> 00:40:17.140
or set it as default or pick
different versions here.

00:40:17.140 --> 00:40:19.020
Again, if you're
thinking about maybe

00:40:19.020 --> 00:40:21.810
you have multiple models you
want to serve for a given use

00:40:21.810 --> 00:40:24.000
case, maybe for
purposes of AB testing,

00:40:24.000 --> 00:40:25.590
different recommendation
approaches.

00:40:25.590 --> 00:40:27.756
The version capability is
really nice to have there,

00:40:27.756 --> 00:40:31.120
because you can pick and choose
what you want to deploy into.

00:40:31.120 --> 00:40:32.270
But now, that's all done.

00:40:32.270 --> 00:40:33.500
We've trained our model.

00:40:33.500 --> 00:40:34.820
We've got it ready to serve.

00:40:34.820 --> 00:40:36.380
We've pushed up to production.

00:40:36.380 --> 00:40:39.340
So the last step in
this process is actually

00:40:39.340 --> 00:40:40.850
getting a prediction out.

00:40:40.850 --> 00:40:44.886
So what I'm going
do here is show you

00:40:44.886 --> 00:40:47.510
what that prediction looks like,
really quickly, on the command

00:40:47.510 --> 00:40:48.010
line.

00:40:48.010 --> 00:40:51.080
So I'm going to need
to do gcloud ML Engine.

00:40:51.080 --> 00:40:53.212
I'm going to predict.

00:40:53.212 --> 00:40:54.670
I'm going to pick
the model I want.

00:40:54.670 --> 00:40:58.480
So in this case, I'm going to
choose the matrix factorization

00:40:58.480 --> 00:40:59.890
model.

00:40:59.890 --> 00:41:02.980
And then for my input,
you have the option

00:41:02.980 --> 00:41:05.350
of supplying either a set
of inputs inside of JSON

00:41:05.350 --> 00:41:08.014
and doing an
asynchronous prediction,

00:41:08.014 --> 00:41:10.180
or you can actually do a
bunch of batch predictions.

00:41:10.180 --> 00:41:12.430
In this case, I'm going to
do a quick synchronous one,

00:41:12.430 --> 00:41:15.400
and because this
data was preprocessed

00:41:15.400 --> 00:41:17.230
using TensorFlow
records, I'm going

00:41:17.230 --> 00:41:20.080
to use a canned TF record that
I had saved off to the side.

00:41:40.570 --> 00:41:42.300
Whoops, thanks.

00:41:42.300 --> 00:41:44.840
Again, typos, stage,
always happens.

00:41:48.182 --> 00:41:49.890
So that's going to
start off and running.

00:41:49.890 --> 00:41:52.050
And it needs to be warmed
up again, because I only

00:41:52.050 --> 00:41:52.650
ran it an hour ago.

00:41:52.650 --> 00:41:55.108
And I have it set to turn off
these things, this production

00:41:55.108 --> 00:41:57.120
model, if I'm not using
it very often to save

00:41:57.120 --> 00:41:58.680
a little bit of money.

00:41:58.680 --> 00:42:01.346
But what it's doing right now is
actually taking that TensorFlow

00:42:01.346 --> 00:42:03.450
record input, and
actually it's running it

00:42:03.450 --> 00:42:06.090
against the Prediction
API that I've decided upon

00:42:06.090 --> 00:42:09.110
and giving me an
actual physical output.

00:42:09.110 --> 00:42:11.480
And of course, nothing works
when you run it on stage.

00:42:11.480 --> 00:42:12.260
There it goes.

00:42:12.260 --> 00:42:13.129
OK.

00:42:13.129 --> 00:42:15.170
So I'm not going to tell
you what all this means.

00:42:15.170 --> 00:42:16.580
But basically,
because of the way

00:42:16.580 --> 00:42:19.750
I structured the input data
into TensorFlow records,

00:42:19.750 --> 00:42:23.870
using this approach, it gives
me a set of scores and output

00:42:23.870 --> 00:42:26.850
classes for a given user, for
a number of different movies.

00:42:30.780 --> 00:42:32.780
So that's a little bit
that end-to-end workflow.

00:42:32.780 --> 00:42:34.154
Can we switch back
to the slides?

00:42:38.060 --> 00:42:39.920
So I want to plug a
couple of things really

00:42:39.920 --> 00:42:42.796
quickly here, because I didn't
do all this work on my own.

00:42:42.796 --> 00:42:44.420
There's just not
enough time to come up

00:42:44.420 --> 00:42:46.460
with this entire
workflow end to end.

00:42:46.460 --> 00:42:49.730
So I definitely leaned heavily
on some of the Cloud ML samples

00:42:49.730 --> 00:42:50.995
from the Cloud ML team.

00:42:50.995 --> 00:42:53.120
So they were nice enough
to have a bunch of samples

00:42:53.120 --> 00:42:54.892
specifically around
this data set that I

00:42:54.892 --> 00:42:56.100
chose which was really handy.

00:42:56.100 --> 00:42:58.640
But I urge you to
check out that repo,

00:42:58.640 --> 00:43:02.062
because nothing beats
actual end-to-end workflow,

00:43:02.062 --> 00:43:04.520
and you can read the docs only
so often before you actually

00:43:04.520 --> 00:43:06.000
need physical steps to try out.

00:43:06.000 --> 00:43:09.180
So these folks have put
together something really useful

00:43:09.180 --> 00:43:11.401
that I would definitely
take a look at.

00:43:11.401 --> 00:43:12.900
And then the other
thing I leaned on

00:43:12.900 --> 00:43:14.940
was a paper that
one of my colleagues

00:43:14.940 --> 00:43:18.031
wrote, "Building Recommendation
Systems in TensorFlow."

00:43:18.031 --> 00:43:20.280
And he actually goes through
a similar approach, where

00:43:20.280 --> 00:43:21.960
he uses MovieLens data set.

00:43:21.960 --> 00:43:23.940
But he also goes all
the way to the end,

00:43:23.940 --> 00:43:27.120
where he talks about things
like how to actually serve

00:43:27.120 --> 00:43:28.590
the model using Cloud Endpoint.

00:43:28.590 --> 00:43:31.170
So you have a little bit more
API management on top of it,

00:43:31.170 --> 00:43:35.410
where you get more logging,
authorization, and monitoring.

00:43:35.410 --> 00:43:38.200
But also talking about
using Cloud Composer,

00:43:38.200 --> 00:43:40.390
and how you actually
update models over time,

00:43:40.390 --> 00:43:42.850
and how you update
this entire workflow.

00:43:42.850 --> 00:43:44.990
Today, for the purposes
of this approach,

00:43:44.990 --> 00:43:48.460
I was the one orchestrating
the data landing in GCS,

00:43:48.460 --> 00:43:51.400
cleanse with Dataprep,
querying at BigQuery,

00:43:51.400 --> 00:43:53.620
and all the way to training
and deploying the model.

00:43:53.620 --> 00:43:56.260
But in reality, you're not
to have a person doing that

00:43:56.260 --> 00:43:57.940
beyond the first
couple of times,

00:43:57.940 --> 00:43:59.980
and that's where Composer
is really your friend.

00:43:59.980 --> 00:44:02.360
And it can help you define
that entire workflow

00:44:02.360 --> 00:44:04.390
and let you run that
on a regular basis.

00:44:04.390 --> 00:44:05.944
Again, as you get
new data in, you

00:44:05.944 --> 00:44:07.360
might want to
retrain those models

00:44:07.360 --> 00:44:08.943
or deploy them in a
different fashion.

00:44:11.980 --> 00:44:14.530
So what does the
typical workflow look

00:44:14.530 --> 00:44:15.947
like inside a Google Cloud?

00:44:15.947 --> 00:44:17.530
And this is sort of
a superset of what

00:44:17.530 --> 00:44:18.530
I've covered here today.

00:44:18.530 --> 00:44:21.520
But it really does go
through the steps of--

00:44:21.520 --> 00:44:24.670
we have all the tools you
need to go through from data,

00:44:24.670 --> 00:44:29.020
to ingest, all the exploration
capabilities, preparing it,

00:44:29.020 --> 00:44:32.759
wrangling that data, maybe doing
some level of preprocessing

00:44:32.759 --> 00:44:35.050
and then getting into the
machine learning components--

00:44:35.050 --> 00:44:37.090
training, hypertuning, testing--

00:44:37.090 --> 00:44:40.300
and then deploying those models
either in online predictions

00:44:40.300 --> 00:44:42.220
or in batch predictions.

00:44:42.220 --> 00:44:45.130
And under the bottom there,
this whole thing lets you--

00:44:45.130 --> 00:44:47.920
can be run by Cloud Composer.

00:44:47.920 --> 00:44:49.480
It's that orchestration layer.

00:44:53.426 --> 00:44:55.300
So that's the bulk of
what I wanted to cover.

00:44:55.300 --> 00:44:57.740
Now, where do you go from here?

00:44:57.740 --> 00:44:59.560
A couple of things
to think about

00:44:59.560 --> 00:45:01.510
as you're looking
into how do you

00:45:01.510 --> 00:45:03.730
adopt these sorts of workflows.

00:45:03.730 --> 00:45:05.410
So for training, I
definitely recommend

00:45:05.410 --> 00:45:07.330
you take a look at GPUs
and maybe even TPUs

00:45:07.330 --> 00:45:09.550
to boost your
training approaches.

00:45:09.550 --> 00:45:13.480
Because just using bare CPUs,
you could throw a lot of cores

00:45:13.480 --> 00:45:16.090
at the problem, but it's not
always the fastest way to go.

00:45:16.090 --> 00:45:18.730
GPUs and TPUs are really,
really purpose-built

00:45:18.730 --> 00:45:20.750
for linear algebra
in the former case

00:45:20.750 --> 00:45:22.514
and specifically
TensorFlow in the latter.

00:45:22.514 --> 00:45:24.430
So that could have a
really significant impact

00:45:24.430 --> 00:45:26.760
on your training timing.

00:45:26.760 --> 00:45:29.250
I like using batch predictions
to fill in historical data.

00:45:29.250 --> 00:45:32.129
You're not going to need to fill
in every single request online.

00:45:32.129 --> 00:45:34.170
So if you want to fill in
some of the prior work,

00:45:34.170 --> 00:45:36.670
you could do that with batch
and that puts a little bit less

00:45:36.670 --> 00:45:39.750
strain on relying on
another API to fill data

00:45:39.750 --> 00:45:42.450
in when maybe customers are
browsing through your site,

00:45:42.450 --> 00:45:45.190
looking for recommendations.

00:45:45.190 --> 00:45:48.140
For machine learning,
what's great too is--

00:45:48.140 --> 00:45:50.480
and just this week
we have talked more

00:45:50.480 --> 00:45:53.652
about using scikit-learn
and XG Boost.

00:45:53.652 --> 00:45:55.610
So you don't necessarily
have to use TensorFlow

00:45:55.610 --> 00:45:56.660
if you don't want to.

00:45:56.660 --> 00:45:58.710
There are other ways
you can get Cloud ML

00:45:58.710 --> 00:46:00.440
Engine to host and
serve your models

00:46:00.440 --> 00:46:02.672
and do that prediction for you.

00:46:02.672 --> 00:46:05.130
And then, the last one we talked
about a little bit as well

00:46:05.130 --> 00:46:07.830
was automating the end-to-end
workflow with Composer.

00:46:07.830 --> 00:46:10.080
There's actually one other
thing I didn't get a chance

00:46:10.080 --> 00:46:13.690
to put on here, which is BQ ML.

00:46:13.690 --> 00:46:14.970
It's a really powerful tool.

00:46:14.970 --> 00:46:16.660
Again, inside of
that same interface,

00:46:16.660 --> 00:46:18.560
where you can query
with standard SQL,

00:46:18.560 --> 00:46:20.710
we've added some extensions
to actually do machine

00:46:20.710 --> 00:46:22.310
learning right then and there.

00:46:22.310 --> 00:46:24.185
So you could do things
like linear regression

00:46:24.185 --> 00:46:25.900
right in line, and
it's really powerful.

00:46:25.900 --> 00:46:28.840
Again, that might not be the
model used in production,

00:46:28.840 --> 00:46:30.790
but every little bit
of work you do along

00:46:30.790 --> 00:46:33.490
the way to reduce the amount
the next person has to do,

00:46:33.490 --> 00:46:35.986
that has an impact on how long
it takes to get to market.

00:46:38.710 --> 00:46:40.850
So what about if you
want to learn more?

00:46:40.850 --> 00:46:42.400
So I definitely urge
you to check out

00:46:42.400 --> 00:46:45.310
all of the analytics and
machine learning products

00:46:45.310 --> 00:46:47.050
that we have inside of GCP.

00:46:47.050 --> 00:46:49.870
It's an incredibly
powerful portfolio,

00:46:49.870 --> 00:46:52.460
and it just continues
to grow regularly,

00:46:52.460 --> 00:46:55.120
especially if you start digging
into where we're investing

00:46:55.120 --> 00:46:56.620
around machine learning.

00:46:56.620 --> 00:46:59.860
We started, I think,
with the pretrained APIs,

00:46:59.860 --> 00:47:02.160
like vision, natural
language, and translation.

00:47:02.160 --> 00:47:04.660
And then we went all the way
to the other extreme, and said,

00:47:04.660 --> 00:47:07.330
we'll run your TensorFlow
models for you at scale,

00:47:07.330 --> 00:47:10.069
and now, you're seeing us invest
a lot more in just the middle.

00:47:10.069 --> 00:47:11.860
What if you don't want
to write TensorFlow,

00:47:11.860 --> 00:47:13.270
but you want a custom model?

00:47:13.270 --> 00:47:15.230
And that's what
Auto ML does for us.

00:47:15.230 --> 00:47:17.534
So there's a lot of work
really happening there.

00:47:17.534 --> 00:47:19.450
And then I would definitely
have you check out

00:47:19.450 --> 00:47:20.860
DA303 later today--

00:47:20.860 --> 00:47:22.480
A Modern Data
Pipeline in Action.

00:47:22.480 --> 00:47:24.520
Again, it takes this work
that I'm talking about

00:47:24.520 --> 00:47:27.430
and gives you a real-world
example from one

00:47:27.430 --> 00:47:29.140
of our customers.

00:47:29.140 --> 00:47:31.570
As I mentioned, check out
BigQuery ML, really cool.

00:47:31.570 --> 00:47:32.690
I think it's a great tool.

00:47:32.690 --> 00:47:34.690
And definitely take
advantage of the free trial.

00:47:34.690 --> 00:47:37.510
You can do things like store
10 gigs of data for free.

00:47:37.510 --> 00:47:39.580
It's out of BigQuery,
and your first terabyte

00:47:39.580 --> 00:47:42.200
of processing or query
processing is free every month.

00:47:42.200 --> 00:47:43.824
So there's a lot of
wiggle room there

00:47:43.824 --> 00:47:45.490
to start playing
around with these tools

00:47:45.490 --> 00:47:46.720
and take advantage of them.

